{"answer":"I’ll explain the system as it’s been used so far: the Lake Monitor role is not permanent; it rotates each week between the fishers. Whoever is the Lake Monitor that week is chosen by a simple draw of lots the night before the first trip—each fisher puts a slip in the pot, we spin, and whoever’s name is pulled takes the Monitor duties for that week. It’s quick, fair, and keeps everyone’s eye on the lake’s volume. The Council Clerk, on the other hand, is appointed by the Community Council itself. The Council meets at the end of each month, and whoever gets a majority vote—usually the fisher who has shown the most fairness in the ledger—becomes the Clerk for the next cycle. The Clerk is then responsible for recording catches, issuing bans when someone exceeds the 10 % cap, and lifting bans when the fisher comes back within limits.

This answer doesn’t change my original proposal; it just clarifies the operational details. I still stand by my 10 % rule with the ban provision.
