{"answer":"An apology is a personal, signed statement from the fisher acknowledging that they returned the excess fish, made a mistake, and commit to following the rule. The fisher writes the apology, signs it, and submits it to the council secretary within 24 h of the trip. The council secretary logs the apology in the ledger next to the fisher’s catch record. Verification comes from the ledger entry, the signature, and a cross‑check of the reported catch against the lake volume and allowable limit. If the fisher’s record shows excess was returned but no apology is logged, or the apology is missing, the council flags the fisher for a one‑trip ban.

The council may also ask the fisher to speak with a council member in a short meeting to confirm the apology if needed.

This does not change any part of the rule itself.","revised_policy":"Each fisher may keep at least 1 kg per trip and remove no more than 12% of the lake’s current volume; any catch beyond that must be returned to the lake. Before each trip, each fisher notes the lake’s current volume. The allowable catch is the greater of 1 kg or 12% of that volume. If the actual catch exceeds the allowable amount, the excess is returned to the lake immediately. After every trip, the fisher records the catch on a community ledger. The community council monitors the ledger weekly; any fisher who fails to return excess fish is subject to a one‑trip fishing ban until the fish are returned and an apology is made. The rule is reviewed every four rounds and the 12% cap is adjusted if the lake’s total volume falls below 80 kg or rises above 150 kg.","revised_operationalization":"The council secretary keeps a digital ledger accessible to all fishers. Each fisher submits a signed receipt within 24 h that lists the lake volume, the allowable catch, the actual catch, any excess returned, and a signed apology if they had to return excess. The council reviews the ledger weekly or via shared drive. Any discrepancy triggers a one‑trip ban until the fisherman returns the fish and submits the signed apology. The council records the apology in the ledger next to the trip entry.","reasoning":"I kept the policy the same because an apology is just a formal, signed acknowledgement. No rule text needs changing."}

