{"answer":"To keep the rule honest, we’ll use the same system that has worked so far: every fisher records their catch on the shared ledger, and we appoint a rotating ‘sheriff’—usually the most experienced fisher who is not on a trip that day—to check the numbers. After a trip, the sheriff walks the boat, looks at the catch bucket, and confirms that the fisher has left exactly 1 kg on board and that the total taken does not exceed 6 kg. The fisher signs the ledger entry, and the sheriff signs a quick verification stamp. If a fisher tries to take more than 6 kg or fails to leave 1 kg, the sheriff will add 1 kg to the communal reserve and, as agreed, the fisher will be barred from the next trip until the violation is corrected. The council, which meets after each fishing day, reviews any withdrawal requests from the reserve, checks that the fisher has indeed been below 1 kg for two straight trips, and approves the 2 kg draw if everything checks out. Everyone can see the ledger, so the process stays transparent and the community can intervene if someone cheats. The enforcement is thus a combination of physical inspection by the sheriff, record‑keeping on the ledger, and council oversight of reserve withdrawals.

